It’s understandable that it ran long the first time you were leading it, but birds sitting crated for hours isn’t something to just shrug off if it can be avoided. Delays happen, but the aim should be to keep crate time as short as possible and make sure ventilation, weather conditions, stocking density, access to water where applicable, and checks for heat stress or injury are properly managed.

For next time, it may help to:

- work backwards from the lorry’s confirmed arrival time rather than starting too early;
- get a realistic loading-rate estimate from the team;
- stage equipment and labour before catching starts;
- keep checking birds in the first-loaded crates, especially in warm weather;
- have a clear plan for what to do if transport is delayed;
- tell the supervisor/manager honestly that the first crates were waiting several hours, so the timing can be improved.

The older lads may be used to it, but “that’s how it goes” shouldn’t replace good welfare practice. You got the job finished, and noticing that the timing wasn’t right is exactly what will help you run it better next time.

It makes sense you tried to choose the most ethical option available. “No riding” is definitely better than places offering rides, shows, painting, etc.—but unfortunately “sanctuary” isn’t a regulated term in Thailand, so it doesn’t automatically mean the elephants’ welfare was excellent.

River bathing is a bit of a grey-to-concerning activity. Elephants naturally bathe, but tourist bathing sessions can be stressful or exploitative if elephants are brought there on a schedule, expected to tolerate groups of people touching them, or controlled with chains, commands, or bullhooks. It can also interfere with their choice to rest, feed, socialize, or avoid people. So the Instagram comment isn’t necessarily wrong—but it also doesn’t mean your particular visit was definitely harmful.

The strongest welfare-focused places generally let visitors **observe elephants at a distance**, with little or no touching, bathing, feeding-on-demand, performances, or forced interaction. Signs that a place was more genuinely ethical include:

- elephants could walk away from visitors and had lots of space;
- no riding, tricks, shows, breeding, or photo props;
- no visible bullhooks, harsh handling, or prolonged chaining;
- elephants lived in compatible social groups and could forage/rest naturally;
- staff focused on rescue, lifelong care, and transparent veterinary/financial information—not just tourist experiences.

Don’t beat yourself up. Marketing around elephant tourism is often intentionally reassuring, and many visitors don’t know that bathing can be part of the same interaction-based business model. For future trips, choosing an observation-only program is the safest standard. If you remember the sanctuary’s name, you could look for independent welfare reviews or reports rather than relying on its own website.
